you're watching the daily climate show  on sky news on today's programme  boris johnson and joe biden meet ahead  of the g7 summit tomorrow with  climate change a key part of the agenda  after a washout may some areas could see  a heat wave this weekend  we find out if climate change is to  blame for our weather getting stuck  [Music]  and the carbon footprint of a cornish  pasty  is revealed  [Music]  hello and welcome to the uk's only daily  climate news show  where we track the changes happening to  our world right now  and meet those developing the solutions  well as world leaders from the g7  countries prepare to meet this weekend  in cornwall  it's expected that the climate crisis  will be a  key area of discussion and ahead of the  official summit which begins tomorrow  boris johnson and the u.s  president joe biden have held talks on  key issues  including the environment well in a  moment we'll hear from  mary robinson chair of the elders on  what might be achieved at this weekend's  meeting  but first sky's dan whitehead has the  latest  there is no doubt that climate change is  right at the top of the agenda here at  the g7 summit the u.s president joe  biden  and uk prime minister boris johnson  holding those early bilateral  meetings today to try and really set the  agenda  over the weekend that all seven leaders  can come to some agreement agreements  when it comes  to the challenges that climate change  presents  and there will be pressure from no less  than 40  groups who are holding events right  across cornwall the likes of ocean  rebellion extinction rebellion  who want to let the leaders know um  about the challenges that the world is  facing  from oil from climate change sea level  rises  i mean simply driving in here to send  ives this afternoon  there are at least half a dozen or so  bridges over the main roads with  extinction rebellion  banners and protesters waving at every  tv crew  so they want that message to get out  there and they'll be making their voices  heard and of course what better  backdrop for the leaders and for the  protesters to get their message across  here in cornwall of course so marine  county  so much marine history here and of  course they are impacted  on a daily basis by some of the  challenges that climate change presents  so we will see those protests over the  weekend and undoubtedly climate change  itself  will be right at the top of the agenda  for the g7 leaders well one person who  knows the inner workings of these  high-level talks better than most  is mary robinson the former president of  ireland is now  chair of the elders the international  organization of global leaders  i spoke to her earlier and i started by  asking what she thought about the cuts  to the uk's foreign aid budget  to put into legislation zero percent  point seven percent  was real leadership and frankly it's  never been needed more  this is the wrong time and the wrong way  especially when you have presidency of  the cop  we need to build trust with developing  countries the hundred billion dollars a  year  um of climate finance half of it going  to adaptation  has been long promised and we still  haven't got there  and meanwhile developing countries are  seeing the huge  solidarity packages that the richer  parts of the world  you know understandably want for their  citizens and are able to do  for their citizens but you know the  contrast between not able to reach the  100 billion it's becoming  an angry point and when you know the the  the presidency of cop has decided  in this year of all years to reduce what  was  leadership and that means cutting  budgets  in yemen cutting budgets in sudan  cutting budgets in syria  you know it's shocking the presidency of  the cop has to have the trust  of developing countries if we're going  to have a successful cop  that's just the absolutely prerequisite  what you're touching on there is that it  needs to go deeper doesn't it than uh  sort of cheerful communique at the end  of the g7 there needs to be a lot of  detail  what detail specifically would you like  to see  i want to see the g7 commit  to every country this year  in glasgow having a nationally  determined contribution which is real  worked out measurable and about  being aligned to the 1.5 degrees so it's  yeah yes it's good to have the long-term  goal of being zero carbon and zero  emissions by uh 2050 um but  we're more interested in what's going to  happen in 2025 2024  2026 you know measurable just as the  vaccines have to be measurable which is  why  the elders really support the gavi  um kovacs uh commitment that was made  for a billion by the first of september  and 2 billion by mid 20  20 22 not saying in a global way we will  vaccinate the whole world  that's kind of rhetoric we need  precision well you can see more of my  interview with mary robinson on our  youtube channel you just head to  youtube.com  sky news well we can take a look now at  some of the day's other climate news  and the prince of wales has hosted a  meeting with ceos from some of the  world's leading companies  ahead of a meeting with g7 leaders he  was joined by the u.s climate envoy john  kerry  and alex sharma the president of cop 26.  well tomorrow the chief executives will  meet the g7 leaders the first  such meeting between government and  global business leaders  to discuss the acceleration of  sustainable investment  and the first collaboration between the  biodiversity and climate bodies of the  un  has said that narrowly focused actions  to combat climate change  can harm nature and vice versa they say  any measures that focus too narrowly on  combating climate change  should be evaluated in terms of their  overall benefits  and risks for example generating  renewable energy can cause  surges of mining activity or use  large areas of land  the seabed off the coast of sussex is  going to be leased from the queen  as part of plans to create a marine park  the water was previously home to a vast  historic  kelp forest and was a haven for marine  life  but 90 of it has been lost over recent  decades  there are also plans to expand the park  along the entire coastline of east  and west sussex  and the coral reef ecosystems could  dissolve  faster than they grow by 2050 that's  according  to a new study reefs have to regenerate  and grow  to survive depositing calcium carbonate  to make  new structures a process called  calcification  calcification rates have declined by  about four percent a year since the 70s  researchers believe the drop is partly  due to ocean  warming and coral bleaching  and a cave painting thought to be the  oldest in the world  is rapidly peeling off due to climate  change  the 44 000 year old artwork on  indonesia's island of  sulawesi is being exfoliated off due to  increasing salt erosion in the cave  scientists say that warming temperatures  alongside prolonged drought combined  with heavy monsoonal rainfall  has created conditions intensifying the  salt crystallization  now temperatures across the uk have been  steadily  rising this week some areas could even  experience a heat wave  this weekend but the warmer weather that  we've been experiencing  is rather different isn't it to last  month which was dominated by low  pressure heavy downpours and  prolonged spells of rain so what  is going on with our weather we're here  to explain more  is kirsty mccabe from the royal  meteorological society also  sky's weather presenter afternoon to you  kirsty and just explain  why is our weather getting stuck and has  it got anything to do with  climate change hello there sam  lots to talk about when it comes to the  weather and weather patterns  and it does seem like maybe we have been  stuck in something of a cool wet me  and then quite a nice warm june and it  is all to do with the jet stream  and now the jet stream is this  fast-moving ribbon of air that's high up  in our atmosphere and it steers low  pressure systems around on the ground  and it's all to do with the temperature  difference between the poles and the  equator  and when there's a large temperature  contrast like we get in the winter then  that means a stronger jet stream and we  see low pressure systems move across the  uk  in summer the contrast is weaker and  that allows the jet stream to move  further north  and we get more settled weather so in  may there the jet stream was a little  bit further south and that's why we got  all that  wet weather when it comes to the links  with climate change it's a little bit  inconclusive at the moment um some  school of thought is that the jet stream  will weaken  because there's not so much of a  temperature difference if the arctic is  warming and it is warming much more  quickly than the rest of the globe then  due to something called the arctic  amplification  then that will reduce the temperature  contrast the jet stream won't be as  strong  however there is also an argument that  with a  warmer world a warmer globe we're going  to see tropical storms moving a little  bit further north into northern  latitudes  and they will interact and strengthen  the jet stream so at the moment  we can't really pin it down to climate  change but when it comes to heat waves  we can now heat waves are pretty much  likely to be linked to climate change  we're going to see more intense and more  frequent heatwaves  with just a small increase in global  temperature it has a big  impact good to see you in your glorious  garden there thank you kirsty thank you  now with the leaders of the world's  seven richest countries meeting in  cornwall tomorrow to discuss the  defining issues of our time  there is one question that remains  unanswered and that is  what is the carbon footprint of a  cornish pasty  well luckily for us a team at the  university of  exeter have been doing the sums and  professor stefan  joins me now to fill us in on their  findings  professor boehm good to talk to you why  are some of the country's finest minds  turning their attention to the humble  cornish pasty  well it's a very popular product 120  million of these are produced each year  here in cold war  and i'm calling uh talking to you from  from cornwall from the penryn campus  and we've teamed up with capacity makers  to  first of all assess what is actually the  carbon footprint of  each uh pasty and we found about 1.5 to  2 kilos per pasty which actually is not  too bad if you compared to  other full meals like a roast dinner  or a lasagna so it's not a bad  carbon footprint but of course it can  still be lowered  particularly if you're thinking about  120 million  times these um it's it's significant  so what are the main drivers of that  carbon footprint  well if you open up a pasty i bought  this early on our campus shop  so you have about um  a whole range of ingredients but a  typical cornish pasty has  passed meat in there beef  um a range of vegetables and of course  the pastries made from  wheat all of that has a carbon footprint  so we've used something called  life cycle analysis to estimate what  the carbon footprint is of all of all of  these ingredients  and of course meat is you know is the  biggest contributor here  uh beef has quite a large carbon  footprint so if you um  take away the meat or you source the  meat differently  you're reared in different ways you have  organic better aquaculture  then you can lower the carbon footprint  quite significantly so we developed a  tool  that allows capacity makers to assess  that  carbon footprint and alter the  ingredients  change the composition of opacity to to  lower the carbon footprint  well i'm sure the world is definitely a  better place for it uh professor  stefan bowen good to talk to you thank  you thanks very much  well that is everything from us for  today but coming up tomorrow we've got a  special extended program  live from cornwall as that g7 summit  gets underway  well you can see that here on sky news  at half past six tomorrow evening  hope to see you then

